/// Reassign every row one principal owns to another.
///
/// THE COUNTERPART TO RANK-STRICT WRITES (memql#4838, epic memql#4832 O1).
/// D3 makes peer rows read-only at every rung -- owner-to-owner included -- so
/// an owner who leaves the company leaves rows no living principal can edit.
/// This closes that, and a break-glass WRITE ESCAPE deliberately does not: an
/// escape is available to everyone who can reach it, which is the whole
/// cluster-owner tier, and it would hollow out D3 on the day it shipped. A
/// transfer names a new owner and leaves the rank rules untouched -- the row
/// simply has a different owner afterward, and every predicate keeps its
/// meaning.
///
/// Cluster-owner only. Refused when the destination principal does not exist
/// or is deactivated, because transferring into a void recreates the problem
/// silently: the rows are still there, still unwritable, now with an audit
/// trail saying they were handed over. Cluster-owned rows (empty owner) are
/// SKIPPED rather than transferred -- `v1:accounts:account:self` has no
/// meaningful second owner, and handing the deployment's own company to a
/// person is a downgrade, not a transfer.
///
/// Audited once for the whole transfer on v1:identity:auditEvent -- the
/// DECISIONS log -- not once per row moved: the decision was taken once, by
/// one person, about one pair of principals.
@description("Reassign every row one principal owns to another. Cluster-owner only; audited.")
@executor("integration.identity.transferRowOwnership")
builtin transferRowOwnership {
  /// The principal whose rows are being handed over.
  fromUserId  string!
  /// The principal receiving them. Must exist and be active.
  toUserId    string!
  /// Narrow the transfer to a single concept. Absent transfers every concept declaring an owned tier.
  concept     string
  /// Narrow the transfer to a single row. Offboarding is per-user; a single stuck row is not.
  rowId       string
}

/// Begin GitHub Connect: answer the URL the browser navigates to, with a server-held state bound to
/// the signed-in caller.
///
/// THE FLOW STARTS OVER THE STREAM, NOT OVER HTTP (epic memql#4912, decision C4). The callback is
/// the only HTTP surface this feature has; everything a signed-in person does goes through the
/// stream like every other call, which is what keeps the state bound to a caller the engine has
/// already authenticated.
///
/// It answers `{authorizeUrl, reason, installUrl}` and never an error a client has to parse. Three
/// reasons a caller can see:
///
///   ok                        -- authorizeUrl is set; navigate to it.
///   github_app_not_configured -- this cluster has no GitHub App (the six MEMQL_GITHUB_APP_*
///                                values are absent). authorizeUrl and installUrl are empty and
///                                the Source stop offers the pasted-token path alone. An
///                                operator's condition, not a person's.
///   connect_state_invalid     -- the state row could not be written. The person retries; there
///                                is nothing for them to fix.
///
/// A call carrying no actor is REFUSED rather than answered, because the state row names the
/// account the callback may land a grant on and a row naming nobody is a grant nobody owns.
@sdk
@executor("integration.identity.githubConnectBegin")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Begin GitHub Connect (epic memql#4912, C4). Writes a short-lived state row bound to the caller and answers {authorizeUrl, reason, installUrl}: navigate the browser to authorizeUrl, and GitHub redirects back to this cluster's identity service, which exchanges the code, seals the tokens and lands one v1:platform:sourceCredential grant of kind github_app owned by the caller. reason is 'ok' on success, 'github_app_not_configured' when this cluster has no GitHub App (authorizeUrl empty; offer the pasted-token path instead), or 'connect_state_invalid' when the state could not be stored. installUrl is the app's installation page, for 'Install on another organization'; empty when unconfigured. Never returns a token, and never echoes the state value anywhere but inside authorizeUrl.")
builtin githubConnectBegin {
  /// Where in MemQL OS to land when the callback finishes -- a same-origin path such as
  /// "/packages/new". Validated as a relative path on the way in and again on the way out; anything
  /// absolute, protocol-relative or carrying a control character is dropped for the OS root.
  returnPath  string
}

// ---- The cluster's GitHub App, registered from the product ----
//
// (design record docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-20-github-app-setup-design.md)
//
// GitHub Connect needs a GitHub App, and an app used to be something an operator
// registered by hand and carried into the deployment as six environment values.
// On a cluster nobody did that for, Connect GitHub answered
// github_app_not_configured -- to a cluster OWNER, whose next step was a runbook
// and a restart. These three let the owner register the app from the product
// instead: GitHub creates it from a manifest this cluster composes, hands the
// credentials back, and the cluster keeps them sealed.
//
// THE FLOW STILL STARTS OVER THE STREAM (decision C4 of the Connect design, kept).
// githubAppSetupBegin binds a server-held state to a caller the engine has
// already authenticated as a cluster owner; the two HTTP routes it leads to
// (owner-approved, identity only) exist because GitHub's manifest flow begins
// with a browser FORM POST to github.com and ends with a browser redirect, and
// neither is something a stream can carry.

/// Say whether this cluster has a GitHub App, where it came from, and whether the caller could
/// register one.
///
/// It exists so a surface can know BEFORE anybody presses Connect. The only way to learn "this
/// cluster has no app" used to be a refused githubConnectBegin, so the Source stop offered a button
/// and then took it back.
///
/// It answers `{configured, source, slug, installUrl, canSetup}`:
///
///   configured -- true when all six values resolve, from one tier.
///   source     -- "environment" (the deployment set them; the product shows the app and changes
///                 nothing), "cluster" (registered from the product; replaceable and removable
///                 here), or "" when there is no app.
///   slug       -- the app's URL slug, which is public: it is the last segment of the app's own
///                 page on github.com. Empty when there is no app.
///   installUrl -- where the app is installed on another account. Empty when there is no app.
///   canSetup   -- the caller is a cluster owner AND the environment is not the one deciding.
///
/// Never a credential: not the client secret, not the key, not the webhook secret, and not the
/// app id or client id either, which nothing in a browser needs.
@sdk
@executor("integration.identity.githubAppStatus")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Whether this cluster has a GitHub App, which tier it resolved from, and whether the caller could register one. Answers {configured, source, slug, installUrl, canSetup}; source is 'environment', 'cluster' or ''. Any signed-in caller; never returns a credential.")
builtin githubAppStatus {
}

/// Begin registering this cluster's GitHub App. CLUSTER OWNER ONLY.
///
/// It writes a short-lived state row (purpose `app_setup`) bound to the caller and answers
/// `{startUrl, reason}`: navigate the browser to startUrl, a page on the identity service whose one
/// job is to post this cluster's manifest to GitHub. GitHub shows the owner the app it is about to
/// create; they confirm; GitHub redirects back to the identity service, which exchanges the code
/// for the app's credentials, seals them, and sends the browser on to install the app.
///
/// THE MANIFEST IS NEVER AN ARGUMENT. It is composed on the server from this cluster's own
/// domain -- the callback URL, the webhook URL, the two read permissions -- so nothing a browser
/// sends can ask GitHub for a wider app than Connect uses, or point its callback somewhere else.
///
/// Reasons a caller can see:
///
///   ok                                -- startUrl is set; navigate to it.
///   github_app_managed_by_environment -- the deployment set MEMQL_GITHUB_APP_*; the app is the
///                                        operator's, and rows written here would be ignored.
///   github_app_setup_forbidden        -- the caller is not a cluster owner.
///   github_app_setup_invalid          -- `organization` is not a GitHub organization login.
///   connect_state_invalid             -- the state row could not be written; retry.
@sdk
@executor("integration.identity.githubAppSetupBegin")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Begin registering this cluster's GitHub App from a manifest. Cluster-owner only; audited. Writes a short-lived state row and answers {startUrl, reason}: navigate the browser to startUrl. reason is 'ok', 'github_app_managed_by_environment', 'github_app_setup_forbidden', 'github_app_setup_invalid' or 'connect_state_invalid'. The manifest is composed server-side and is never an argument.")
builtin githubAppSetupBegin {
  /// Where in MemQL OS to land when the round trip finishes -- a same-origin path, validated on
  /// the way in and again on the way out exactly as githubConnectBegin's is.
  returnPath    string
  /// The GitHub organization to register the app under, by login. Absent or empty registers it
  /// under the account of whoever is signed in to GitHub in that browser.
  organization  string
}

/// Remove the GitHub App this cluster registered from the product. CLUSTER OWNER ONLY.
///
/// It clears the six stored values, so Connect is absent again and every source fetching under a
/// grant answers github_app_not_configured until an app is registered. It does NOT delete the
/// app at GitHub -- GitHub offers no call for that, and the owner's next step, if they want it
/// gone there too, is the app's own settings page.
///
/// Refused with github_app_managed_by_environment when the deployment set the values: there is
/// nothing stored to clear, and clearing rows would change nothing about what the cluster uses.
@sdk
@executor("integration.identity.githubAppRemove")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Remove the GitHub App registered from the product: clears the six stored values. Cluster-owner only; audited. Answers {removed, reason}; reason is 'ok', 'github_app_managed_by_environment', 'github_app_setup_forbidden' or 'github_app_not_configured'. Does not delete the app at GitHub.")
builtin githubAppRemove {
}

// ---- Groups (epic memql#5165, section D) ----
//
// Six verbs, all executed by integrations/groups and all writing under
// internal origin: v1:identity:group and v1:identity:groupMembership are
// UNOWNED rows (D2), so there is no owned tier for a caller-reachable mutation
// to write through, and every guard below is a Go check against the caller's
// AccessContext rather than something a mutation body could state.
//
// The guards are stated in the design record's table and enforced in
// integrations/groups/guards.go. Each refusal is a TYPED CODE the OS keys its
// copy on, which is why they read as identifiers rather than sentences.

@sdk
@executor("integration.groups.groupCreate")
@description("Create a custom group, optionally tied to an account. Requires `create` on `group`; a named account must exist and be active. Returns {groupId, name, accountId, kind, status}. The group's KIND is always \"custom\" -- the account-kind group is written by the ensureAccountGroup automation and never by a caller, so a person cannot mint a second one and split an account's membership across two rows.")
builtin groupCreate {
  /// Display name, as a person would say it.
  name         string!
  /// What the group is for. Optional; group pickers fall back to the name.
  description  string
  /// The v1:accounts:account this group grants. Omit for a group that grants nothing and exists to organize (D8).
  accountId    string
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.groups.groupUpdate")
@description("Rename a group or change its description. Requires `update` on `group`. Refuses `group_not_active` on an archived group -- an archived group grants nothing, and editing one reads as reviving it. Returns {groupId, name, description}.")
builtin groupUpdate {
  groupId      string!
  /// New display name. Omit to leave it.
  name         string
  /// New description. Omit to leave it.
  description  string
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.groups.groupArchive")
@description("Archive a group and remove its memberships. Requires `update` on `group`. Refuses `group_account_active` on an account-kind group whose account is still active (D5) -- that group is the account's, and archiving it alone would leave the account with no way for its people to reach its work while still looking configured. Archive the ACCOUNT instead, and the cascade takes the group with it. Returns {groupId, status, membershipsRemoved}.")
builtin groupArchive {
  groupId  string!
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.groups.groupMemberAdd")
@description("Place a person in a group. Requires `update` on `group`, refuses `group_self_add_refused` when the target is the caller (nobody adds themselves -- D7), and refuses `group_member_rank_not_below_caller` unless the target ranks STRICTLY below the caller. Re-adding somebody who was removed writes a new version at the same derived id rather than a second row. Returns {membershipId, groupId, userId, origin, status}.")
builtin groupMemberAdd {
  groupId  string!
  userId   string!
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.groups.groupMemberRemove")
@description("Remove a person from a group. Requires `update` on `group` OR that the target IS the caller -- a person may always take themselves out, which is the one asymmetry with groupMemberAdd and is deliberate (D7). Otherwise the same rank rule applies. Returns {membershipId, groupId, userId, status}.")
builtin groupMemberRemove {
  groupId  string!
  userId   string!
}

// ---- The account lifecycle's two (epic memql#5165, D5) ----
//
// THESE TWO CARRY THEIR GATE AS AN ANNOTATION, and the reason is the one epic
// memql#5288 established: a top-level builtin call returns from the engine's
// own branch before the plan-level refusal runs, so the builtin executor asks
// the question itself (component/memql/requires_capability.go,
// refuseBuiltinBelowRequiredCapability). It runs BEFORE the handler, which is
// what makes a refusal say nothing about whether the named account exists.
//
// THE ABSENCE OF @sdk IS NOT A GATE. It is a generator marker with no engine
// effect -- it keeps a name out of sdk/go and sdk/ts and out of nothing else.
// The capability below is what decides who may drive these, and
// component/memql/group_account_builtin_capability_test.go pins both halves.
//
// INTERNAL ORIGIN PASSES, which is the point rather than a loophole. Every
// caller in this tree is the engine -- the ensureAccountGroup and
// archiveAccountGroup automations (dsl/accounts/automations.memql) and the
// seed materializer's boot backfill -- and trusted server-side Go stamped for
// one call is not a principal. A gate that refused them would leave an
// archived account's people still holding its groups.

@requiresCapability("create", "group")
@executor("integration.groups.groupEnsureForAccount")
@description("Ensure the account-kind group for one account exists, at the derived id acct-<accountShortId> (D5). Requires `create` on `group`, the verb groupCreate checks, because this writes a group row -- and the account-KIND one groupCreate itself refuses to mint. Called by the ensureAccountGroup automation on account creation and by the seed materializer's boot backfill for every active account -- so it must be idempotent, and it is: a re-run on an account that already has an active group writes nothing. NOT @sdk: this is the engine placing a row, never a person. Returns {accountId, groupId, created}.")
builtin groupEnsureForAccount {
  accountId  string!
}

@requiresCapability("update", "group")
@executor("integration.groups.groupArchiveForAccount")
@description("Archive every group tied to one account and remove their memberships -- the archiveAccountGroup cascade (epic memql#5165, D5). Requires `update` on `group`, the same verb groupArchive checks, because this is that archive taken over every group one account has. BOTH KINDS, not just the account-kind one: a custom group tied to an archived account grants access to a client that is gone, so leaving it active would mean the archive changed what the screens show and not what the people can reach. NOT @sdk -- the cascade is the engine's. Returns {accountId, groupsArchived, membershipsRemoved}.")
builtin groupArchiveForAccount {
  accountId  string!
}
